*** Obsolete docs ***


The CHIME master control program ch_master.py is designed to be run as a daemon
(i.e., in the background).

INSTALLING
----------

Do:
  sudo python setup.py install

(This does the following:
- Compiles the extension module chrx.
- Installs all modules required for ch_master.py into system paths.
- Creates /etc/CHIME if it doesn't exist.
- Puts the configuration files in /etc/CHIME, and also puts the current git tag
  in /etc/CHIME/version.
- Puts an upstart script in /etc/init
- Installs ch_master.py in /usr/sbin.
)

If you are a developer and want a local install for development, do instead:
  sudo python setup.py develop


RUNNING
-------

Ch_master.py runs as a daemon using upstart, the standard daemoniser for Ubuntu.
Normally, once installed, you just want to do:
  sudo start ch_master

To stop, do:
  sudo stop ch_master

To see if it is running, do:
  sudo status ch_master

Of course, you can still run from the command line in the git path, but this
should only be done for development purposes. In fact, ch_master will force you
to enter the git tag if you do this (see VERSION CONTROL, below).


LOOKING AT LOG FILES
--------------------

When you run ch_master.py as a daemon (as you normally should), there is no
output to the screen. You must look at a log file.

To view the log file, there are a few options. Upstart automatically logs to
/var/log/upstart/ch_master.log, but is only readable by root. A log file is
placed in the current acquisition directory (e.g.,
/data//20130726T001446Z/20130726T001446Z.log). The current directory also has a
symbolic link, so easier is to look at /data/ch_master.cur/*.log.


VERSION CONTROL
---------------

In the future it might be useful to know which version of ch_acq was being used.
This information is recorded as follows:

When you install (see INSTALL, above), a file called /etc/CHIME/version is
created which has the current git version (git describe --tags). When
ch_master.py is started as a daemon, the program reads this version number and
inserts it in the header of the HDF5 file.

If you do not run ch_master.py as a daemon (which you would not do unless
developing), you must use the "-g" option and manually specify which git version
you are using. In fact, ch_master.py will not start unless you do this. This
option parameter is then written to the HDF5 header.
